    {
      "id": "JGGmZVIMeyLlHm3RQ6bn-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 577.0999463663026,
      "y": -124.27766564137724,
      "width": 379.48535712245217,
      "height": 2625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aQ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1232501328,
      "version": 529,
      "versionNonce": 425303619,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792252726523,
      "created": 1792237975080,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- aqua culture harms (photoplancton)\n\n- chlorophyll, heat, pH,\n\n- Micro Algae, Macro Algae\n- Collect data later for better\nprediction\n\n-Problem:\nCoastal businesses in Iceland are\nexposed to environmental changes\nthat can disrupt their operations and\ngenerate significant economic losses.\nIn particular, harmful algal blooms can\nthreaten aquaculture production by\ncausing fish mortality, reducing\nharvestable biomass and forcing\ncompanies to make costly emergency\ndecisions. Meanwhile, the accumulation\nof seaweed and other algal biomass\nalong coastal areas can increase\ncleaning and maintenance costs for\nhotels and tourism businesses,\npotentially reducing the\nattractiveness of beaches and\nwaterfront facilities. Companies\ncultivating or processing algae for\nbiocompost, biofertilizers and other\neco-friendly products also depend on\nmarine conditions that influence\nbiomass availability, growth and\nproduct quality.\n\nDespite the availability of satellite\nobservations, oceanographic\nmeasurements and marine\nenvironmental forecasts, businesses\nmay lack timely, location-specific\ninformation to anticipate these\nevents and assess their potential\nimpact. This uncertainty makes it\ndifficult to plan operations, allocate\nresources efficiently and implement\npreventive measures before\nenvironmental conditions become\nproblematic.\n\n- Value proposition:\nAn AI-powered, location-specific\nenvironmental risk and decision-\nsupport platform.\nWe integrate satellite observations,\noceanographic forecasts, local water-\nquality measurements and historical\nbloom records to estimate the\nprobability that a harmful algal bloom\nwill develop or reach a specific\naquaculture site. The platform sends\nearly warnings with predicted risk,\nuncertainty, expected arrival time and\nrecommended response options, helping\noperators make better-informed\ndecisions before conditions become\ndangerous. \n\nTarget customers:\n- Aquaculture operators:\nProblem: Harmful bloom exposure can\nthreaten fish welfare, biomass and\nharvest plans.\nProduct: Farm-specific alerts, bloom\narrival forecasts, risk levels,\nsupporting evidence and estimated\nfinancial exposure.\n- Algae producers and processors:\nProblem: Environmental variability can\naffect seaweed growth, biomass yield,\nharvest timing and product quality.\nHarmful blooms may also create\ncontamination or processing concerns,\ndepending on the species and intended\nproduct.\nProduct: Site suitability forecasts,\ngrowth-condition monitoring, harvest\nplanning and harmful-event or\ncontamination alerts where relevant.\n- Hotels and municipalities: They have\nto pay for cleaning up the beaches\nand coastlines\n\nBusiness model and revenue potential:\n- Paid pilot: A limited-duration\nengagement for one farm or fjord,\nincluding retrospective analysis,\nforecast testing and performance\nreporting.\n-Annual subscription: Site-specific\nmonitoring, forecasts, automated\nnotifications, historical analytics and\nreporting.\n-Enterprise service: Multi-site\nmonitoring, integration with farm\nsystems, custom alert thresholds,\nincident analysis and economic-risk\nreporting.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- aqua culture harms (photoplancton)\n\n- chlorophyll, heat, pH,\n\n- Micro Algae, Macro Algae\n- Collect data later for better prediction\n\n-Problem:\nCoastal businesses in Iceland are exposed to environmental changes that can disrupt their operations and generate significant economic losses. In particular, harmful algal blooms can threaten aquaculture production by causing fish mortality, reducing harvestable biomass and forcing companies to make costly emergency decisions. Meanwhile, the accumulation of seaweed and other algal biomass along coastal areas can increase cleaning and maintenance costs for hotels and tourism businesses, potentially reducing the attractiveness of beaches and waterfront facilities. Companies cultivating or processing algae for biocompost, biofertilizers and other eco-friendly products also depend on marine conditions that influence biomass availability, growth and product quality.\n\nDespite the availability of satellite observations, oceanographic measurements and marine environmental forecasts, businesses may lack timely, location-specific information to anticipate these events and assess their potential impact. This uncertainty makes it difficult to plan operations, allocate resources efficiently and implement preventive measures before environmental conditions become problematic.\n\n- Value proposition:\nAn AI-powered, location-specific environmental risk and decision-support platform.\nWe integrate satellite observations, oceanographic forecasts, local water-quality measurements and historical bloom records to estimate the probability that a harmful algal bloom will develop or reach a specific aquaculture site. The platform sends early warnings with predicted risk, uncertainty, expected arrival time and recommended response options, helping operators make better-informed decisions before conditions become dangerous. \n\nTarget customers:\n- Aquaculture operators:\nProblem: Harmful bloom exposure can threaten fish welfare, biomass and harvest plans.\nProduct: Farm-specific alerts, bloom arrival forecasts, risk levels, supporting evidence and estimated financial exposure.\n- Algae producers and processors:\nProblem: Environmental variability can affect seaweed growth, biomass yield, harvest timing and product quality. Harmful blooms may also create contamination or processing concerns, depending on the species and intended product.\nProduct: Site suitability forecasts, growth-condition monitoring, harvest planning and harmful-event or contamination alerts where relevant.\n- Hotels and municipalities: They have to pay for cleaning up the beaches and coastlines\n\nBusiness model and revenue potential:\n- Paid pilot: A limited-duration engagement for one farm or fjord, including retrospective analysis, forecast testing and performance reporting.\n-Annual subscription: Site-specific monitoring, forecasts, automated notifications, historical analytics and reporting.\n-Enterprise service: Multi-site monitoring, integration with farm systems, custom alert thresholds, incident analysis and economic-risk reporting.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
